[
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-05",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-05",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Overview and justification",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>By now you should be comfortable with the basics of referencing: naming sources, matching a style, and spotting an inconsistent citation. This course builds on that. It moves from getting referencing right to judging it: checking a paraphrase properly against its original, weighing whether a reference list's range reflects the real scope of someone's research, and working out how to reference a source that does not fit a standard template.</p><p>By the end you will be able to check a paraphrase against its source, judge whether a reference list's range reflects real research scope, paraphrase a technical passage for a non-specialist reader, work out what an unfamiliar source needs, reference it correctly under time pressure, and gather and cite sources that both support and challenge a position.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-10",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-10",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Learning outcomes",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>By the end of this course, you will be able to:</p><ul><li>Analyse a paraphrase against its original source to check that no distinctive wording survives unattributed.</li><li>Evaluate a reference list to judge whether its range, including opposing views, reflects the genuine scope of the research undertaken.</li><li>Paraphrase a technical passage accurately for a non-specialist reader, marking any direct quotation and crediting the source throughout.</li><li>Analyse an unfamiliar source type to identify which elements a reader needs to locate it, rather than forcing it into a standard template.</li><li>Reference an unfamiliar source type correctly under time pressure, checking guidance and recording the decision so the approach can be repeated.</li><li>Gather and cite sources that both support and challenge a position, so a reader can judge the breadth of research behind a recommendation.</li></ul>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-15",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-15",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "slot__wrapper-open__practice-connection",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "In real academic practice",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>Checking a paraphrase, judging a reference list's range, and working out how to cite something unfamiliar are not one-off exam skills: you will meet them across every module that asks for independent reading, from literature reviews to group projects with mixed source types. The judgement you build here also feeds directly into how you handle sources when researching and when writing critically, where the same questions about range, attribution and traceability come up again.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-20",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-20",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Signpost",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The course has six short lessons. First, checking a paraphrase against its original source. Second, judging whether a reference list's range reflects real research scope. Third, paraphrasing a technical passage for a non-specialist reader. Fourth, working out what an unfamiliar source type needs. Fifth, referencing an unfamiliar source correctly under time pressure. Sixth, gathering and citing sources that support and challenge a position. Work through them in order; each builds on the one before.</p>",
    "instruction": "Work through the six lessons in order.",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-25",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-25",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Course instructions",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p><strong>Start and finish with a confidence check.</strong> Before the lessons, you'll rate how confident you feel about each part of this topic. You'll rate the same statements again at the end, so you can see what has changed. Your ratings are yours: they aren't marked and no one else sees them.</p><p><strong>The activities in the lessons are for practice.</strong> Questions, examples and reflective prompts are there to help you think, and they don't count towards passing. Reflective prompts are private and optional.</p><p><strong>The test at the end is the one that counts.</strong> To complete the course you need to take the final test and score at least 70%. You can retake it.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-c-30",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-OPEN-b-30",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "How this course works",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The examples and activities in this course use invented people, authors and sources to show how things work. Unless we say otherwise, any name, citation or reference you meet in an example is fictional.</p><p>Where a lesson draws on real work, you'll find it listed under <strong>Lesson Sources</strong> at the bottom of that page. Every real source used in the course is gathered under <strong>Course Sources</strong> at the end.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
