  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-c-05",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-b-05",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Course summary",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>You have reached the end of the course. Referencing is no longer just about getting the mechanics right: you can now check a paraphrase against its source, judge whether a reference list's range reflects the real scope of someone's research, paraphrase a technical passage for a non-specialist reader, work out what an unfamiliar source needs, reference it correctly under time pressure, and gather sources that support and challenge a position. That judgement builds directly on the accuracy and consistency you brought from the introductory course. Look back at the confidence check to see how far this has moved.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-c-10",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-b-10",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "slot__wrapper-close__practice-connection",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "In real academic practice",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>Apply this judgement the next time you draft a literature review or a group report with mixed source types: check your own paraphrases against their originals, weigh whether your reading list's range reflects the scope of the question, and work out an unfamiliar source's citation from first principles rather than forcing it into a template. The same judgement about range and traceability is exactly what you draw on when finding and using sources for a longer project, and when researching a topic that calls for a documented method.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-c-15",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-intermediate-W-CLOSE-b-15",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Where this goes next",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>This course has assumed the introductory groundwork: naming sources accurately, matching a style, and spotting an inconsistent citation. If any of that still feels shaky, the introductory course is there to revisit. Moving on, the advanced course in this pathway turns from getting the judgement right to acting on it under pressure: the ethical choices that referencing and attribution raise once you are working independently, in shared work, or in a professional role.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
